# 5. Unreal-side binding model

## 5.1 Binding asset

Create a `UGameWebUIBindingAsset`.

Example asset:

```text
MainHUD.bindings
```

It contains selected sources, state bindings, and event bindings.

Conceptual editor view:

```text
MainHUD Binding Setup

Sources:
  Player       -> BP_PlayerCharacter
  Weapon       -> BP_WeaponComponent
  HUDState     -> BP_HUDState
  Interaction  -> BP_InteractionComponent
  Combat       -> BP_CombatComponent

State:
  Player.Health
  Player.MaxHealth
  Weapon.CurrentAmmo
  Weapon.CurrentSpread
  HUDState.CrosshairMode
  Interaction.bCanInteract
  Interaction.Prompt

Events:
  Weapon.OnPlayerFiredShot(GunType, AmmoRemaining, Spread)
  Combat.OnHitConfirmed()
  Player.OnTookDamage(Amount, Direction)
```

The user selects those through UI dropdowns.

The asset should store stable references to:

```text
source alias
source class
property path or property FName
delegate/event path or FName
optional web-facing alias override
optional preview/range override
optional update/tolerance override
```

## 5.2 Source aliases

Bindings are usually designed against classes, but runtime needs actual object instances.

At runtime, a bridge registers objects against the aliases used by the binding asset.

C++ example:

```cpp
WebUI->RegisterSource(TEXT("Player"), PlayerCharacter);
WebUI->RegisterSource(TEXT("Weapon"), WeaponComponent);
WebUI->RegisterSource(TEXT("HUDState"), HudStateObject);
WebUI->RegisterSource(TEXT("Interaction"), InteractionComponent);
WebUI->RegisterSource(TEXT("Combat"), CombatComponent);

WebUI->LoadBindings(MainHUDBindings);
```

Blueprint equivalent:

```text
Register Game Web UI Source
  Source: Player
  Object: BP_PlayerCharacter instance

Register Game Web UI Source
  Source: Weapon
  Object: BP_WeaponComponent instance

Load Game Web UI Bindings
  Binding Asset: MainHUD
```

The C++ API may still accept `FName` internally, but the normal user workflow should be picker-driven.

